## 2. Component Rules

### Always functional — never class components

```typescript
// Good
const EmployeeCard: React.FC<EmployeeCardProps> = ({ employee, onClick }) => (
    <div className="rounded-lg border p-4" onClick={() => onClick(employee)}>
        {employee.fullName}
    </div>
);

// Never
class EmployeeCard extends React.Component<EmployeeCardProps> { ... }
```

### Props: typed, destructured, with defaults

```typescript
type EmployeeCardProps = {
    employee: Employee;
    isSelected?: boolean;
    onClick: (employee: Employee) => void;
};

const EmployeeCard: React.FC<EmployeeCardProps> = ({
    employee,
    isSelected = false,
    onClick,
}) => { ... };
```

### Size limit: ~150 lines per file. Split if larger.

### No anonymous default exports

```typescript
// Good
export const EmployeeCard: React.FC<...> = ...;
// Avoid
export default ({ employee }) => ...;
```

---

## 3. TypeScript Rules

- Strict mode always on
- No `any` — use `unknown` and narrow, or define the type
- No non-null assertion `!` — handle nullability
- Prefer `type` for props and local shapes; `interface` for extensible contracts
- Enums for fixed status sets

```typescript
// domain.types.ts
export type EmployeeStatus = "Applied" | "Interview" | "Offer" | "Rejected"

export type Employee = {
    id: string;
    fullName: string;
    email: string;
    status: EmployeeStatus;
    projectId: string;
};
```

New components: `.tsx`. Existing JS components: maintain as `.js` until planned migration.

